{
  "abstract": "We study common-future descent certificates for the accelerated Collatz map $U(n)=(3n+1)/2^{v_2(3n+1)}$. If a forward word uses only division exponents one and two and contains no consecutive twos, its initial value is the unique smallest positive odd ancestor of the endpoint at the same odd-step depth. Every reversed suffix is minimal as well. The proof compares inverse paths through an integer affine relation $y=2^\\delta x+c$ and the invariant $0\\le c\\le5(2^\\delta-1)$; a congruence modulo four resolves its boundary case. For divisible-by-three starts whose word begins with one, we prove that a smaller ancestor requires at least four additional odd steps. This is sharp on $n\\equiv1275\\pmod{2592}$, where $U^3(n)=U^7((64n-33)/81)$. We also give an exact expanding-prefix word with adjacent twos that admits a smaller ancestor at the same depth, showing why the structural restriction matters. Independent integer checks support the proofs, and a separate symbolic checker verifies the two entire arithmetic-class certificates. These results provide sound pruning rules for inverse searches; they do not cover all positive starts or prove the Collatz conjecture.",
